# Canvas

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A canvas is an area of a page that a script paints on, pixel by pixel. Custom cards use it for graphics that ordinary page elements cannot draw. Home Assistant does not offer a canvas card or a canvas component of its own. You meet it when a custom card draws on one, and inside some of Home Assistant’s own chart cards.

This page covers:

- Canvas
  - `<canvas>`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

A canvas has a size in pixels (300 by 150 by default) and a drawing that a script creates. It can hold fallback content between its tags, and that content can be read by assistive technology. The drawing itself cannot be.

<%= example('cv0-canvas-layers') %>

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-canvas"><code>&lt;canvas&gt;</code></h3>

The element is written by a custom card’s code, not by a dashboard author in YAML or Markdown. A Home Assistant user cannot write one in a Markdown card, so there is no snippet to copy here.

What you can control as an author of a custom card:

- **Size.** The width and height set the pixel size of the drawing. CSS can scale it, but a scaled drawing looks blurred unless the code allows for screen density.
- **Colour.** Theme variables do not reach pixels that are already drawn. The code has to read the theme colours when it draws, and draw again when the theme changes.
- **A name.** Give the canvas a label or point it at a visible heading, and put the same information in text.
- **Keyboard.** A canvas is not focusable by default. If it responds to touch, give it a keyboard alternative.

A theme cannot colour a canvas, change its text size or make its drawn text wrap. Text drawn onto a canvas does not grow when people increase text size or zoom, and it does not reflow on a narrow screen.

For a graphic that must follow the theme, use SVG, which can take its colours from the page, or a chart card. See [Charts and tables](charts.html).

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- It can draw many moving or detailed shapes quickly.
- Its contents are invisible to a screen reader, so every fact in it needs a text copy.
- Theme colours, text size and dark mode do not apply to it unless the code handles them.
- Animation can be distracting, and it can be unsafe if it flashes.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use a canvas only when SVG or a chart card cannot do the job. Always write a short summary beside it and add a table or list of the same values, as in the example. Give the drawing a name, or mark it with a role as an image and a label.

Avoid animation. If you need it, let people stop it, and never flash more than three times a second. Do not put the only copy of any information in drawn text.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.1.1 Non-text Content: a text alternative for the drawing.
- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: a table for data.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour, 1.4.3 and 1.4.11 Contrast: choose and measure the colours you draw.
- 1.4.4 Resize Text and 1.4.10 Reflow: drawn text does not resize.
- 2.1.1 Keyboard and 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value for any drawing that responds to input.
- 2.2.2 Pause, Stop, Hide and 2.3.1 Three Flashes for animation.


